 ### [Emission Reconstruction](14424-emission-reconstruction.md)
-Emission reconstruction is a differentiable inverse volume rendering technique that solves for the spatial distribution of emitted radiance ($L_{emit}$) within a volume such that the rendered output matches target images. It operates by linearizing the volume rendering equation under the assumption of single scattering, isolating the emission term from scattering and background contributions to form a linear system. Unlike [Albedo Reconstruction](14422-albedo-reconstruction.md), which optimizes the scattering color properties, this method treats emission coefficients as the primary unknowns.
+Emission reconstruction is an inverse volume rendering technique that solves for the spatial distribution of emitted radiance ($L_{emit}$) within a volume such that the rendered output matches target images. It linearizes the volume rendering equation under a single-scattering assumption, isolating the emission term from background and scattering contributions to form a linear system. Unlike [Albedo Reconstruction](14422-albedo-reconstruction.md), which optimizes scattering color properties, this method treats emission coefficients as the primary unknowns.
 
 ### Extinction Reconstruction
-Extinction reconstruction linearizes the volume rendering equation by assuming constant outgoing radiance $L_m$ and applying a logarithm to the transmittance term. It starts with the expanded rendering equation, separates the background and medium contributions, and exploits the constant $L_m$ to integrate the medium term into $L_m (1 - e^{-\int \sigma_t dt})$. The background term is rewritten using basis functions. This method is restricted to cases where $L_m$ is constant, but this is typically acceptable as extinction defines the volume shape and is often derived first [S1].
